  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 155 AM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...  A Flood Watch will be issued for a good portion of \nCentral NC, except for the Northwest Piedmont (Triad Area) east \nalong the NC/VA border. Timing most likely will be 16z today through \n12z/Saturday.\n\nThe main synoptic boundary was located over our region early today, \nanalyzed from near the Tidewater of VA southwest through the \nnorthern Triangle Area and southwest to Charlotte. A couple of \nclusters of showers and thunderstorms have been developing ENE along \nthe boundary from CLT to near RDU. GSP recently had Severe \nThunderstorm Warning around Charlotte. Another, much larger complex \nof thunderstorms was located over NE Alabama into GA and \nsouthwestern NC, moving NE. Current PWs were running between 1.8 and \n2 inches along and south of the boundary, with a max of 2.2 over NE \nAL. This complex should continue moving NE as the mid/upper trough \napproaches the southern Appalachians this morning. PWs will increase \nto 2 to 2.5 inches along and ahead of the feature (boundary). \n\nWe will place the Flash flood watch along and south of the expected \naverage placement of the boundary today. The latest CAMS suggest it \nwill drift southward through 12z, possibly sagging through the \nTriangle. By 18z... it should be somewhere near a line extending \nfrom near Rocky Mount southwest to around our south of Raleigh and \nsouthwest to around Wadesboro. As the mid level trough approaches, \nthe boundary may waver back north 25 to 50 miles as a surface wave \napproaches this afternoon. So, the Flood Watch would be favored \nmainly south of I-85/I-40 east to near Wilson (southward). Several \nwaves of showers/thunderstorms are expected along and south of the \nboundary, with showers north. The highest QPF should be found within \nthe Watch area, with 2-4+ inches likely - mostly in 3-6 hours. Some \nlocal 5+ inch amounts will be possible. \n\nMost guidance (which again has been unreliable thus far) suggests \nconvection will taper off this evening, with additional \nshowers/storms possible again later tonight (mainly south and east \nportions of the current watch. We will see. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... There is a Slight Risk of severe thunderstorms \ntoday into this evening, mainly over southern portions of central NC.\nThis is surrounded by a marginal risk (near and south of the \nboundary). \n\nMorning cloudiness and convection should taper off along and south \nof the aforementioned boundary. There should be a rapid development \nof new showers/storms with the approach of the mid level support \nlate this morning or early afternoon. SPC has upgraded southern \nportions of our region into the slight risk category as the \npotential for wet microbursts has increased. Strong heating in the \nwarm sector will lead to very unstable conditions ahead of the wave \nof low pressure and cold front. Storms will spread east through the \nwarm sector, with the threat of some 60 mph winds and associated \ntorrential rainfall this afternoon.",
